ry of the sami people in northern  europe  she is also part of the kanai first  nation blackfoot confederacy  her films include the multi-awarded  documentary short rebel  which is autobiographical and the  feature the body remembers when the  world broke open  which she co-wrote co-directed and acted  in  it premiered in 2019 at the toronto film  festival receiving three awards  including  best picture and at the berlin film  festival  lijia villalobos has had a long career  as both studio executive  and a filmmaker her many film credits  include writing and executive producing  the independent feature  la mismaluna also known as under the  same moon  writing the film firelight produced by  alicia keys and mary martin  and consulting on pixar animated  features including coco  firelight was nominated for an naacp  image award and received a 2013  humanitas prize  lulu wong first gained international  attention for writing and directing  posthumous in 2014  and then again in 2019 for her second  feature  the farewell which she wrote directed  and co-produced  the farewell follows struggling new york  city artist billy played by aquafina  who travels to china to visit her dying  grandmother wong filmed the farewell in  the neighborhood where her own  grandmother actually lived  and she cast her great aunt liu hung as  herself in the film  last year variety named wong one of 10  directors to watch and aquafina won a  golden globe for her performance  the farewell also won the independent  spirit award for best film  we look forward to hearing your  conversation everyone bird take it away  thank you and i want to thank everyone  for joining us especially our filmmakers  our screenwriters you know  it's i feel so honored to be um here  having a conversation with you  i myself am just kind of logging in  essentially from  uh gabriela no tongva lands and tatavium  lands which hollywood is built upon  i don't think that's a fact that's very  well known within many communities uh  but it's very i'm very honored to come  here to you today but i also have to  mention that  i myself you know descend from  matrilineal and matriarchal indigenous  nations and so i feel very humbled  myself to be speaking with  such four powerful women such as  yourselves and i think the great voices  that you  you bring to your work and to our  screens and to  audiences around the world so i just  want to first of all say thank you for  what you give and what you do i wanted  to you know just basically kind of start  our conversation by  i guess kind of asking you um i think as  a point of view  on our conversation like i wonder if  somebody could kick us off just by  telling us how they would kind of  describe  what they've defined as their own  authentic voice that they're lending to  their work does somebody want to kick us  off with that  lulu do you want to start um sure  yeah i think it you know it took me a  really long time i think i'm still  in the process of discovery of what that  means to  to have a voice and to  have a voice in your work uh so for me  for a long time it was finding um  the juxtaposition of pathos and humor  which for me is very much at the  heart of my family my  culture the way that i see the world the  way that my family has taught me to see  the world  so humor was a big part of um  like finding you know the kind of humor  in my work that i wanted to  show was a big part of my process of um  you know of it landing because you know  humor is so specific  to every culture and it may not be  easily translatable  so that was a really difficult thing  when i set out especially to make the  farewell to say oh this is funny and if  someone goes  well is it funny you know and how do you  like  break that down right um because what  someone finds funny  it just is and so that was  a big um exploration  of uh this particular type of humor and  especially with a visual medium like  film like for me it was  really a journey to figure out how to  actually represent that kind of humor  on screen so that it translates to  not only like to audiences but through  multiple different cultures  that's great thank you what about you  i think for me the thing that i always  have to keep going back and forth  about is how i see myself on the inside  which is a hundred percent mexican  and how i need to see myself in the  entertainment industry  which is as a latina  and you know for me a latina  the word latino or latinos and now  latinx for example  it's not really a word that we ever use  in the countries that we come from  you know we never say we're latinos we  always say we're mexican or we're  argentinian or we're colombian or we're  brazilian you know  but in hollywood because we have so  little power  and because our stories are seen  so rarely a little bit more in  television now but  almost non-existent in film  unless the movie is coming from another  country so i'm talking about  the ability for us who live in this  country to be able to tell our stories  that is almost non-existent in this  country  the only way that we can sort of have  um some kind of power is by uniting  with the word latina  or latinx or latino but for  me i will always be a mexican  that's how i see myself that is my heart  and so it's navigating that back and  forth and finding specificity  so that we don't just see a  latinx character in television and film  but that we actually bring in the  specificity  of what that character may bring based  on where they came from  or you know what their heritage is  right thank you for that nissan what  about you  um i think my i mean what i've always  wanted is to really expand what it means  to be black  on screen that um i found very  uh early on that um we were  you know as a black writer i was only  really allowed to  um inhabit quite  you know small spaces that there was a  lane that i was allowed to be in  um which didn't which was about the the  you know you look at the kinds