hings that i i  say and i'm in the  equity and inclusion committee for the  writers guild of america  um and we have dealt with a lot of  issues of not just sexual harassment by  but harassment and um  racism in the writers rooms and you know  one thing that i always say  is writers who get a show  for the most part are being hired  because they are  talented writers they are incredible  storytellers  they are not getting hired because they  have leadership  skills but they are put in positions  where they have to be leaders of not  only the writer's room  but an entire crew and  a whole group of actors and nobody has  given them  any kind of information or classes  or leadership seminars of  how do you run a crew of 300 people  what is acceptable behavior and not  what is considered racist or not  who can help you navigate  these worlds and these stories and these  people and so now more than ever i think  it's super important because we no  longer just have  four networks with 30 shows being put on  the air every year  we have dozens of cable networks and  streaming services  in addition to network television  we have 500 shows in production a year  so we have a whole generation of writers  that are being put  in these positions with absolutely  no knowledge of how to lead and so a lot  of the issues that i think we  see also in feature sets by the way  because it's not like directors are  giving leadership skills  either but more so i think in television  because there's just so many shows that  are being produced a year  that i think it's vital to  as part of getting hired that you are  actually receiving  information and training on on these  issues  as well as just leadership you know  uh some leadership skills that can guide  you through a process of running a room  and beyond  yeah i think that's important i i agree  with the issue of training because i  one of the things about i think alimaya  has a really interesting story  of when she was you know co-dire