rward and to  understand that  um one key way of dismantling these  these systems of  of oppression and systems of power um  that that that really shape the kinds of  stories that are being told on screen is  to  um is to build capacity within the  indigenous film community  itself thank you i think that's such an  interesting model to contemplate i think  because  if we continue to we have to acknowledge  i think in this moment in time you know  i think given our pandemic and given you  know the state-sponsored terrorism  that's happening against black people in  our country  you know it's like they're starting to  recognize you know these systems of  power and white supremacy that exist not  only in our  political systems in our country but i  definitely think that exists within our  industry  um and i think you know and if we're  gonna if we're gonna continue to  you know have an impact and to create  change i think those systems need to be  you know questioned more and that  they're in that people need to be  trained more lulu um you were just  talking about you know uh having staffed  a writer's room  can you describe kind of like how how we  all  should be getting into the room like how  did you get into the room in order to  get to this point where you are  um i mean honestly i've never written  um in a room before so i i  was told by a couple of my writers they  said  you know this is unlike any room i've  ever experienced and i think  in some ways because i didn't experience  the hierarchy and sort of um  the abuse that's so common in writer's  rooms  um i didn't know to bring i said you  know we're a team  here's a group of people who have very  different backgrounds  very bit different experiences um  who are all tremendously talented so why  wouldn't we  just talk about everything you know i  had to actually  teach um some of the writers who have  been in a lot of rooms and rooms where  they're not allowed to speak up  um they would like you know one wom